::: {.doc-subject}

Check whether the graph has an Eulerian path.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75388-L75477){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.has_eulerian_path(source=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Has Eulerian path](../assets/graphs/has-eulerian-path.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: the house of Santa Claus, a square with both diagonals and a roof; badges give each node's degree and only the bottom corners D and E are odd (outlined), so one stroke can draw it and has_eulerian_path() returns True. Right: without the roof (dashed), all four corners have degree 3 (shown in the accent color), so has_eulerian_path() returns False." width="100%"}

Returns `True` if some trail uses every edge exactly once: either a closed circuit
(`is_eulerian()`) or an open path between two nodes (`is_semieulerian()`). With
`source=`, it checks for such a trail starting at that node, which for an open path
means the node must be one of the two ends (on an undirected graph, an odd-degree node;
on a directed graph, the node with one more out-edge than in-edges).

Isolated nodes are ignored, as in `is_eulerian()`. NetworkX instead requires the whole
graph to be connected, so results differ only for graphs with isolated nodes.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optionally require the path to start here, as an integer ID or a string label.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if an Eulerian path exists (from `source=`, if given).
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `source=` does not exist.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~eulerian_path`) 

:   The path itself.

[](`~is_semieulerian`) 

:   Check for a path but no circuit.

[](`~is_eulerian`) 

:   Check for a circuit.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path can be walked end to end, but only from one of its ends:

::: {#f32839ca .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")])

(
    path.has_eulerian_path(),
    path.has_eulerian_path("A"),
    path.has_eulerian_path("B"),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Where the walk can start

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. With every land mass odd, no walk crosses
each bridge exactly once. Closing the Honigbrücke leaves only the banks odd, so the walk
exists, and starts on one of them:

::: {#7e1157df .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
closed = ec.konigsberg().delete_edge("Kneiphof", "Lomse")

(
    ec.konigsberg().has_eulerian_path(),
    {
        start: closed.has_eulerian_path(start)
        for start in ["North Bank", "South Bank", "Kneiphof", "Lomse"]
    },
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(False,
 {'North Bank': True, 'South Bank': True, 'Kneiphof': False, 'Lomse': False})
```
:::
:::


::: {#abd64fae .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
closed.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight=["North Bank", "South Bank"],
    label_by="label", edge_label_by="bridge",
)
```

